00:01:47.475 --> 00:01:49.500
what is a threat environment?

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.620
And so we start off with a
very high level view of that,

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.110
and it's kind of the category
of who are the people

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.450
or systems that may attack you?

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.420
And those could be
everything from hackers

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.150
for profit on the internet,
foreign governments,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.880
though most likely it's people
inside your organization

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.140
who screw things up.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.529
They accidentally
press RMRF.star

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.090
and delete everything.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.100
That is still a threat
that you have to deal with.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.600
And then similarly, in addition
to knowing who your threats are

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.000
or who the attackers might
be, understanding kind

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.142
of what targets are available.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.600
If you have a database publicly
accessible on the internet,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.060
that's an obvious
target, but there

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.770
may be some other
less obvious targets.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.050
There have been a lot of
interesting hacking attacks

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.640
where someone had
a password written

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.190
on a post it note that was
in the back of a photo.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.870
That's potentially a target
that you have to deal with.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.840
And then lastly, what
tools might be used.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.652
If you understand
who is attacking you,

00:02:41.652 --> 00:02:43.110
the targets that
they're attacking,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.160
and the tools that
they're using,

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.340
you have a much better
way of understanding

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.433
how you can defend
against those threats

00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:51.100
because that's what
you're trying to do.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.860
You're not trying
to outrun everyone.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.840
You're trying to outrun
the other slow people.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.312
Your security has to be
better than everyone else.

00:02:57.312 --> 00:02:59.520
And that's hopefully why
you're coming to Serverless,

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.680
because you get a
bunch of that for free

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.600
and then you configure
the little bits on top.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.650
So this is also one
of my favorite comics.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.200
Hopefully everyone here
reads a lot of xkcd.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.928
But there's kind of this hacker
world of, oh God, the laptop

00:03:13.928 --> 00:03:14.470
is encrypted.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.110
We need to write
a new algorithm.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:16.650
Do it.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:20.460
But the reality is it's
a $5 wrench that gets

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.660
the password on the laptop.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.278
And so that's kind of this
idea of threat models.

00:03:24.278 --> 00:03:25.820
You want to optimize
for what's going

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.080
to happen commonly and
defend against that, rather

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.870
than those kind of scenarios
that maybe you dream of but are

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.900
low probability.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.010
So this talk is actually
kind of modeled off

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:40.330
of a couple of top 10 threats
that we see in Serverless.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.080
So the OWASP Serverless Top 10
and the Cloud Security Group

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.030
Serverless Security Top 12.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:48.610
And those are kind of
the obviously top threats

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:49.450
that they see.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.042
There's a lot of
overlap between these

00:03:51.042 --> 00:03:53.000
and we're going to go
through a number of them.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.780
So things like what
malicious code is

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.540
and where you might get it
and potential for remote code

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.960
execution, denials of service,
storing your credentials

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.300
insecurely, and
then everything down

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.390
to even this idea of poor
developer experience.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:09.690
If your developers have
a manual tool chain,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.460
they are copying things around,
there's a much higher chance

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.380
that something's going
to go wrong which is

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.610
a threat to your environment.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.338
So we're going to start off
with this idea of a zero day.

00:04:19.338 --> 00:04:21.130
So how many people have
heard of zero days?

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.060
Everyone in the room.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:22.840
Exactly.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.450
So why does it matter?

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.730
Why is a zero day so bad?

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:29.230
Typically you have
this thing called

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.710
the window of vulnerability.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.890
So a vulnerability is disclosed.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:35.370
So like Specter or Meltdown.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.030
We get an email that's
like, oh my god,

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.810
this terrible thing happened.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.110
There was a patch out.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.430
You can go and fix
using this patch.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.588
And that's where this kind
of danger zone begins.

00:04:45.588 --> 00:04:47.380
Everyone knows that
this is a vulnerability

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.800
and it can be exploited.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.120
But ideally you can patch things
quickly enough that that danger

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.790
zone is relatively small,
that window of vulnerability

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:56.650
is relatively small.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:58.510
The problem with
zero days and really

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.370
kind of any unknown
vulnerability

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:03.370
is that that danger zone
is now much, much larger.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.740
There is an x point active
somewhere in the wild.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.300
Maybe it's a remote
code execution

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.670
in your function or
some insecure credential

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.050
that you don't know about.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.410
And until you discover
it, kind of this t

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.450
equals 0 all the way up to
the present where it's active,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.740
that's a very, very
dangerous place.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:23.830
And why this is
important, is those things

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.470
could occur anywhere
in your stack.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.860
So if you're building
on top of some VM,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.680
I have a server, a
Raspberry Pi at home running

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:33.730
things on the internet.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:36.940
I'm responsible for hardware
and network on that.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.228
I have to patch the
operating system when

00:05:39.228 --> 00:05:40.520
something like Specter happens.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.430
I'm responsible for that.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.230
I am running a node
server on top of that.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:46.390
I'm responsible for
updating node whenever

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:48.477
it gets their security patches.

00:05:48.477 --> 00:05:50.560
And then I'm also responsible
for the dependencies

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:54.250
that I'm pulling in in my app,
in addition to my app code.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.040
And that's a lot for me to
think about when my Raspberry PI

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.370
server is controlling
my light bulb.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.580
And it's relatively low risk
if my light bulb gets hacked,

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.150
but it's a lot of stuff
that I have to worry about.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.500
And I would like it obviously
if my light didn't turn on

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.462
at random times.

00:06:10.462 --> 00:06:11.920
And so the whole
goal of Serverless

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.860
is to reduce what
you're thinking about.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.820
Shove all of those
potential vulnerabilities--

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.820
the zero days that are going
to wipe out your application--

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:24.752
because you're not worried about
updating the operating system.

00:06:24.752 --> 00:06:26.710
You're not worried about
updating the hardware.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.810
You're not even worried about
updating the language runtime,

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:29.500
in many cases.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.370
You're just focusing
on what matters to you

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.995
and your business, which is the
application code that you write

00:06:33.995 --> 00:06:37.870
and the dependencies
that you're pulling in.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.270
So we do a couple of things
for you in this world, right?

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.790
If you're using Cloud Functions,
App Engine, or Cloud Run,

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.735
we are automatically doing
a bunch of those upgrades.

00:06:45.735 --> 00:06:47.110
So, in the Cloud
Function's case,

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.152
things like your function
framework-- so the code

00:06:49.152 --> 00:06:51.520
that actually wraps that
little function that you write.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.620
The language runtime
and operating system.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:54.700
Same on App Engine.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.598
Language runtime and
operating system.

00:06:56.598 --> 00:06:59.140
And then on Cloud Run-- so Cloud
Run is our newest serverless

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:02.650
containers product where you can
actually provide an arbitrary

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.290
container image, which could
have some crazy operating

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.520
system, you could be pulling
in native dependencies.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.370
We don't know, so
we can't do kind

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:14.225
of the same operating system
level things potentially yet.

00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:16.100
There is a world where
we're looking at, hey,

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.220
we'll give you a
Google Ubuntu base

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.470
image that we are
automatically upgrading

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:22.720
or a Google Python base image
that we're automatically

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:23.680
updating.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.080
So stay tuned for more
of that, of how we make

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.253
that world a little better.

00:07:28.253 --> 00:07:29.670
And then there's
another question,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.830
and it was actually
asked downstairs

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.700
in our serverless
panel earlier today.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.330
We are running a
bunch of containers

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.423
under the hood for
all of those products,

00:07:38.423 --> 00:07:40.590
and we're running them in
a multitenant environment.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.410
That's how we can scale to
10,000 containers in five

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:44.055
seconds.

00:07:44.055 --> 00:07:46.680
And we're doing that because we
run on this massive multitenant

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:47.880
infrastructure.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.980
And there's a really cool
product called gVisor.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.820
So gVisor is an
open-source kernel--

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.660
you can go to gvisor.dev--

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:55.410
and it basically
intercepts all of the sys

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:59.190
calls from the developer
untrusted container,

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.290
and then would remap
them or disallow them

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:04.380
so that you can't have
people going and messing

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:05.970
with other containers.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.030
We also have a thing called
GContain, or GContainer, that

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.520
does kind of the same idea,
but for memory and CPUs

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.820
so you can't have
noisy neighbors

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:20.650
taking up other resources on
that multitenant environment.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:21.150
OK.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.592
So that's kind of base
what Google does for you.

00:08:24.592 --> 00:08:25.800
Let's talk about another one.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.840
So malicious code and
remote code execution.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.600
So it turns out, there
are lots and lots

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.320
of ways to get bad code.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.770
The internet is a
very scary place.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.049
Number one, just don't trust
anything that pulls code

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:38.230
from pastebin, right?

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:40.313
Like, there have been a
number of vulnerabilities.

00:08:40.313 --> 00:08:42.510
There was a great one
last year in ESLint

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.850
where someone checked
in code they just

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.830
pulled from an
arbitrary pastebin URL,

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.960
and then evaled that
code and wrote it

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.030
to another pastebin URL.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:55.470
Those are kind of obvious ones,
but there are some other--

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.660
having other low
quality dependencies

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:58.870
that may not be as obvious.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:01.410
Such as, maybe there
are performance bugs

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:04.920
like they do crazy regular
expression parsing that end up

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.230
causing performance issues.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.510
Or another really common one is
you have multiple environments,

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.720
and so you have a
development environment where

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.890
your developers can
do whatever they want,

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:15.990
and then you have a
production environment

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.190
that's more locked down.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.368
And maybe if you're
sharing those environments,

00:09:19.368 --> 00:09:20.910
dependencies will
make their way over

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.610
from development through
testing and into production,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.540
and maybe they don't have the
performance or the security

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:30.300
requirements that you have.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.660
So how do you know about any
of those vulnerabilities,

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:33.290
by the way?

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:35.420
If you're using an
open-source package,

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:37.970
how would you have any
idea if it was vulnerable?

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:39.470
So there are a bunch
of three letter

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.720
acronyms that I'm going to
throw out, and, by the way,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.637
feel free-- yes, take
photos so that you don't

00:09:43.637 --> 00:09:45.110
have to remember them all.

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:46.130
So CVEs.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:48.380
I'm sure a lot of people
have heard of these.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:51.230
So it is kind of
a way of tracking

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.930
these common vulnerabilities
and exposures,

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.740
and they do so by assigning
every CVE a number.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.500
And so CNAs provide
numbering for those,

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:05.390
so you'd see CVE12345 is x.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:08.300
And so open-source project--
so Node is a great example

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:09.030
of this--

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:12.950
they have the Node CVE
Numbering Authority.

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:15.920
So every single Node
issue gets a number,

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.110
goes into their tracking
system, and then

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:20.730
you can look it up using these.

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.820
So these are kind of the
open-source databases

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.700
for taking a look at,
hey, what vulnerabilities

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.090
may be out there.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.390
There are also a
bunch of other tools

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:32.450
if you're more in this space
that have kind of scraped this

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.610
and provided it a better world.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:37.970
So things like
Greenkeeper or Sneak

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.850
are great ways of just, hey, I
want to actually kind of make

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.130
sense of this, and
even potentially check

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:44.713
my dependencies locally.

00:10:44.713 --> 00:10:46.130
And then I think
we'll have a demo

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.440
later of doing this as well.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:49.940
Cool.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.023
So denials of service,
another attack that I think

00:10:52.023 --> 00:10:53.690
a lot of people have heard of.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.810
So a classic denial of service
that everyone thinks about

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:59.600
is you have some fixed
number of resources,

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:02.000
you have external
traffic coming in,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.100
and now maybe you have
malicious traffic.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.720
And so that traffic
starts coming in

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.390
and it overwhelms the
resources, and your machines

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.030
start backing up and failing.

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:13.010
So serverless
actually introduces

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:16.370
another several relatively
interesting denial-of-service

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:17.060
attacks.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:19.370
You can have the traditional
resource exhaustion

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:23.630
where hey, your function or your
app only has two gigs of RAM

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:26.810
and you'll run out of RAM, or
you only have so much network

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:28.640
throughput.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.340
But because we're
focusing more on code,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.820
you could have some other really
interesting ones of code errors

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.530
that maybe it was
a correct request,

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:39.140
but due to some
regular expression bug,

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:41.330
now you end up with
resource exhaustion.

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:42.830
And then my personal
favorite, which

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.400
is very new for serverless.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.690
Server infrastructure
scales infinitely, right?

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:51.453
Hopefully you saw that demo
of 0 to 10,000 instances.

00:11:51.453 --> 00:11:53.620
I've profiled it, and that's
know a million requests

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:54.415
a second.

00:11:54.415 --> 00:11:55.790
The thing that
will run out first

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:57.897
is your credit card, right?

00:11:57.897 --> 00:11:59.480
You're going to hit
that and you don't

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:02.390
want a $10,000 bill
or $100,000 bill,

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:03.950
and things will start breaking.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:06.830
And so that's this
financial denial of service

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.267
that serverless is so great--

00:12:09.267 --> 00:12:10.850
it's so great on one
side, and results

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.850
in this interesting
problem on the other.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:17.200
So really common one here,
again, with functions, we

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:20.830
will start scaling
up and maybe there's

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:22.390
this impedance mismatch.

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:25.818
So for those of you who
have used SQL databases,

00:12:25.818 --> 00:12:27.610
you can run out of file
descriptors, right?

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.890
You have so many machines
on the front end trying

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:33.190
to connect to this and you'll
exhaust your resources.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:35.920
So we provide a
really nice tool that

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.940
allows you to set a maximum
number of those functions that

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:40.880
are running.

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:42.640
So functions are
single concurrency,

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.270
they're handling one request.

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:47.080
So you can actually say,
I want four instances

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.570
because I know that I have a
backend resource that can only

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:51.490
handle four concurrent
connections,

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:53.150
or whatever the number is.

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:54.850
And we can set that upper bound.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:56.740
And what will happen
now is we will

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:58.850
start getting
external traffic, we

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:00.525
will start spinning
up resources,

00:13:00.525 --> 00:13:01.900
and then, above
that, we'll start

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:04.838
kind of load-shedding that
and applying back pressure.

00:13:04.838 --> 00:13:06.880
And so we'll start queuing
up all those requests,

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:08.080
we're not going to drop them.

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:09.670
But we will just
start processing them

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:11.643
so you only get four at a time.

00:13:11.643 --> 00:13:13.060
So it's a nice way
of, again, when

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:15.160
you have this impedance
mismatch of serverless

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:17.620
can scale infinitely, but
maybe the backend resources

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:20.440
that you're using can't,
you can use max instances

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.720
to try and solve that.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:25.720
It's worth noting that apps and
containers running on Cloud Run

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.880
are multi-concurrent
by default. So I

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:29.990
mentioned functions
are single concurrency,

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:32.290
so they're doing one
request per instance.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:35.210
Apps will actually do multiple
requests per instance.

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.810
So if you're running
App Engine or Cloud Run,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.660
we will actually start
sending those requests back

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.573
to your application.

00:13:42.573 --> 00:13:43.990
So for Cloud Run,
for instance, we

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:47.360
say you get concurrency
limit of 80 by default.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.050
So every instance,
assuming it doesn't run out

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:52.990
of memory or CPU,
it's kind of bound

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.302
to 80 concurrent requests.

00:13:54.302 --> 00:13:56.260
And obviously, you can
put that higher or lower

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:57.800
depending on what you need.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:01.360
But just something to
know, if you really want,

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.180
you could set your Cloud Run
app to single concurrency

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:05.560
and go back to that model.

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:08.740
But we give you
that flexibility.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:09.250
OK.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:10.870
Insecure credential storage.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.628
Don't store passwords
in plain text.

00:14:12.628 --> 00:14:14.170
I feel like I don't
need to say that,

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:16.003
but it still happens,
especially when people

00:14:16.003 --> 00:14:17.447
write really small things.

00:14:17.447 --> 00:14:19.030
There are a lot of
places where you're

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:21.060
going to encounter credentials
and you may need to store them.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.560
So storing database username
and password, right?

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:24.868
I see it all the time.

00:14:24.868 --> 00:14:26.410
Maybe you're doing
OAuth credentials,

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:29.385
so you're signing a user in and
you're storing access tokens.

00:14:29.385 --> 00:14:30.760
You're doing some
kind of crypto.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.590
Maybe you have symmetric
keys, or you're

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:34.450
storing asymmetric keys.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:37.390
Or, similarly, maybe you
have publication credentials.

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:41.290
So publishing your code
to GitHub or deploying it.

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:43.670
We'll talk about the most
common one that I see.

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:46.060
So storing secrets
in code, right?

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:48.190
You end up with, we're
connecting in our function

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.490
to a mySQL database
and someone says, hey,

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.290
here are the user
name and password.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.180
And your function code
can leak, maybe you

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:57.520
accidentally make your
GitHub repo public

00:14:57.520 --> 00:14:59.080
and everything is bad.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:02.380
Someone can just connect to this
database and take your data.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.690
So maybe you want to do
something like storing secrets

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:05.732
in environment variables.

00:15:05.732 --> 00:15:07.750
That kind of solves
one of the problems of,

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:09.700
hey, let's just set
the database user

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.250
to root and store it there.

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:13.890
And your code
looks a lot better.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:16.720
It just says, hey
process.env.DB_USER,

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:17.712
DB_PASSWORD.

00:15:17.712 --> 00:15:19.420
Now, if this gets
public on the internet,

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:21.503
it's fine because you're
binding those credentials

00:15:21.503 --> 00:15:24.977
in when you actually deploy
in the environment variable.

00:15:24.977 --> 00:15:27.310
But there are actually a bunch
of other potential issues

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:28.270
in that world.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:29.853
We'll actually, I
think, see an attack

00:15:29.853 --> 00:15:32.220
on that at the end in a demo.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:34.600
And so you may want to
consider things like Cloud KMS.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.300
So Cloud KMS will
let you do encryption

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:39.150
of, say, a file
in GCS, and so you

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:40.900
can store all of your
secrets in that file

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:42.390
and then pull them out.

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:45.490
Or similarly, if you want to
use a tool like HashiCorp Vault,

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:47.710
maybe you're already
running an instance of that

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:49.430
within your own company.

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:52.680
We also have a link there if
you want to run Vault on GCP.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:56.765
And those are more secure
ways of storing secrets.

00:15:56.765 --> 00:15:58.390
There's a really
great blog post by one

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:02.210
of our developer advocates,
Seth, on how to do those.

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.110
So I think he writes a
bunch of go functions

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:05.610
and a bunch of
Python functions that

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:08.890
show how to do a number
of different secrets

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:13.340
in serverless type of scenarios.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:14.810
Cool.

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:17.080
So those are all--

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.840
some of them are unique to
serverless, but most of them

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:22.990
are just general
application security things.

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:24.730
Execution flow
manipulation, I think,

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:27.800
is much more unique to
serverless, and here's why.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:31.690
So we can imagine that I'm
building a simple function that

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:32.890
deploys--

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:36.380
that writes some content
to a cloud SQL instance.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:38.470
But the reality is
these are microservices,

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:40.178
so I'm going to actually
break them apart

00:16:40.178 --> 00:16:42.535
into the smallest unit of
whatever they are doing.

00:16:42.535 --> 00:16:43.660
So that's one line of code.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.698
So maybe I want to
save some content,

00:16:45.698 --> 00:16:47.990
maybe I have a markdown file
and I'm writing it to HT--

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.350
and transforming to
HTML and then saving

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:52.643
the HTML into a database.

00:16:52.643 --> 00:16:54.310
So I'll have one
function that's, hey, I

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:55.750
want to process some content.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:58.000
And that does markdown to
HTML and then one thing

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.930
that actually saves the HTML.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.940
So you can imagine
the happy path here

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.381
was just straight through.

00:17:04.381 --> 00:17:06.089
My one function calls
my second function,

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:08.500
my second function
calls the database.

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:10.500
But in this case,
these functions

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:12.839
are public on the internet,
people can just kind of

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:15.420
call around them, and you may
get a malicious call that's

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:19.710
like, hey, I just want to save
some arbitrary data that's

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:21.493
maybe not HTML, or
maybe not markdown,

00:17:21.493 --> 00:17:23.160
or anything that kind
of doesn't respect

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.619
the flow of the application.

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:27.310
That's a problem.

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:30.120
So we've introduced it,
actually, in Cloud Run.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.960
You can already go
and use it today.

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:34.123
And in Cloud Functions,
it's an alpha.

00:17:34.123 --> 00:17:35.790
We're still working
on rolling that out.

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:38.405
It's kind of hard to add to a
generally available product.

00:17:38.405 --> 00:17:39.780
But what it does
is it allows you

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:43.050
to specify cloud IAM
policies on your functions

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:44.350
and your services.

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:47.190
And so that would allow you to
authenticate developers or end

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:50.190
users or the other
services, be they

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:53.310
Cloud Functions or Cloud Run
services, or on-prem things

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:55.770
that you're authenticating
with a service account.

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:57.720
And the other big change
here is that services

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.720
will become private by
default. So in Cloud Run,

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.460
when you deploy your Cloud Run
instance, it's already private,

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:04.750
and you have to make it
public manually by adding

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:05.910
a policy down there.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:09.630
So that --allow-authenticated
sets all users

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.270
can invoke this service.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:13.900
Similarly with functions.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:16.060
So what does it look like?

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:18.670
We can have our first
function, that process-content

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:21.640
function, say, I want to
allow anyone to invoke me.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.550
So we're going to say, all users
get the cloudfunctions.invoker

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:25.690
role.

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:27.130
And on our second
function, we're

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:30.160
actually going to say, OK,
only this first function can

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:30.898
invoke me.

00:18:30.898 --> 00:18:33.190
So that provides a little
bit of security where someone

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.170
can't come around, all right?

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:36.850
So we've now
secured that channel

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:39.020
between our two functions.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:42.460
So when someone tries to come
in and save data directly,

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:47.010
IAM is like, nope, we don't
allow that, you can't do it.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:49.230
But, for your
developers, they can just

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.480
say, hey, I want to curl
this using an identity

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:53.190
token that I'm
pulling from G Cloud

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:56.310
or that I've generated via some
other tool, and they can go

00:18:56.310 --> 00:19:00.290
and actually test
that out if they want.

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:02.040
And what about other
cloud services?

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.920
So maybe it's not a human
user that it's calling

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.440
your process-content functions.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:10.350
Maybe you have a scheduled
job, or you have task queues,

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.810
or you have data
coming from Pub/Sub.

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:17.130
So each of those products, Cloud
Scheduler, Tasks, and Pub/Sub,

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:18.900
can get a service
account with a role.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:23.070
So that could be Cloud
Function Invoker, or similarly.

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:25.860
And they can then make
authenticated requests out

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:30.000
to GCF or Cloud Run or other
services on the internet.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.910
And it's both secured
in transit via SSL,

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:35.220
but then also, it
gives that identity

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.010
token that can be
validated by the IAM stuff

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:39.400
that we just talked about.

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:44.130
So it takes that token along,
they validate it, awesome.

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:46.360
And, as I mentioned, also
available in Pub/Sub,

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:49.170
you can do authenticated push
subscriptions out of Pub/Sub.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:50.670
So it's really,
really nice for kind

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:54.110
of creating this very
secure environment.

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:54.610
OK.

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:56.200
And then I actually saw
a question on the Dory,

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:58.140
so hopefully whoever
asked this is here.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:00.730
There is a question about
network restrictions.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:04.000
So currently, only App Engine,
of those three products,

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.650
offers firewalls.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:08.905
So actually, Cloud Functions and
Cloud Run don't yet offer them.

00:20:08.905 --> 00:20:10.780
They are not yet integrated
with Cloud Armor,

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:13.210
but that is something
that we're looking into.

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:15.323
In Google App Engine,
you can say, hey,

00:20:15.323 --> 00:20:16.990
I do want to create
a firewall, and that

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:20.740
would be if you know that there
is some known malicious actor,

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:21.830
you can blacklist that IP.

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.830
Or maybe you are operating
within an environment

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:25.890
where you need to
whitelist certain ones.

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:29.410
Otherwise, strongly recommend
identity-based security.

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:31.540
A lot of large companies
that I've talked to,

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:35.420
do this strong outer wall
and then no security inside.

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:37.420
And that's obviously not
good because if someone

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:40.577
does penetrate that outer wall,
they can do whatever they want.

00:20:40.577 --> 00:20:42.910
And identity-based security,
kind of going back to those

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:44.380
granularity--

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:48.310
to a granularity thing,
sort of makes the barrier

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:49.850
feel a little more permeable.

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:52.630
But generally, every service
has their own little wall

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:56.170
around it, which makes
that more secure.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.880
It's all trade-offs
in engineering.

00:20:58.880 --> 00:20:59.560
OK.

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:03.040
So continuing on, the
ways of authenticating

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:04.170
your application.

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:05.920
We see, a lot of times,
that people end up

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.230
with overprivileged
identities in those functions.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:11.770
So by default, every
function shares

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:14.560
the same identity and that
identity has the editor role.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.270
So it can actually
go and spin up VMs

00:21:16.270 --> 00:21:17.770
and do a bunch of things.

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:19.520
And that's basically
because we said, hey,

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.937
we know that people want to
build functions to demo things

00:21:21.937 --> 00:21:24.250
really quickly, they
should be able to do that

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:27.886
without having to learn all
the complexities of IAM.

00:21:27.886 --> 00:21:29.612
But we then said,
OK, well, obviously,

00:21:29.612 --> 00:21:31.570
if you're deploying these
things in production,

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:33.278
you need this concept
of least privilege,

00:21:33.278 --> 00:21:35.350
where your function
can only access the one

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:36.790
thing that it needs.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:38.200
And so that is
where this concept

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:40.160
of per-function identity
comes in and says,

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:43.150
OK, we know that this function
needs exactly this role,

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:44.983
we don't want it
shared with anyone.

00:21:44.983 --> 00:21:46.900
And that's how we actually
talked a little bit

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.023
earlier about how our save--

00:21:49.023 --> 00:21:50.440
or our processContent
function can

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:54.130
talk to our saveData
function, and only those.

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:56.680
So we can walk through
an example of that.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:00.010
I can create a service
account called processContent,

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:05.410
and I can say, OK, on Cloud
Functions, I'm going to grant--

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:07.450
on that saveData function,
I'm going to say, OK,

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.570
serviceAccount.processContent@
whatever gets the Cloud

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:12.520
Functions invoker role.

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:15.040
And then I can
deploy my function

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:16.600
using that service account.

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:19.990
So what I've done is I've
said, OK, on functionA,

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:23.590
you get this specific role, I'm
going to deploy you with that.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:28.600
And on functionB, that
function can invoke it.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.480
And then we can do the
same thing, actually,

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:31.480
for our second function.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:33.540
So our second function
is talking to a Cloud SQL

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:34.340
instance.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:37.210
So we can say, OK, we'll give
you the saveData function,

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:39.462
or we'll give you a service
account called saveData

00:22:39.462 --> 00:22:40.920
and we'll do the
same thing, right?

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:44.400
We'll say, hey, on the project,
give you the Cloud SQL client

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:47.880
role so you can actually create
a connection to Cloud SQL.

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:51.740
And then we will deploy
our function using that.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:54.110
So now, with
per-function identity,

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:58.200
functionA can talk to
functionB, and functionB

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.090
can talk to our database.

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:01.620
And really, the
important thing here

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:05.280
is functionB can't go and talk
to any other thing, right?

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:08.670
It can't go out and talk to
GCS or any of the other cloud

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:13.310
services because it
doesn't have that access.

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:15.720
And what about things
within network restrictions?

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:18.970
So maybe you're not running
a Cloud SQL instance.

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:21.270
Maybe you're running
some other database

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:24.390
that doesn't integrate
well with Cloud IAM,

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.130
but it's within a VPC.

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:29.760
So you're running that on cloud
and we obviously can't just

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:32.460
make an HTTP
request to your VPC,

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.230
you can use a VPC connector.

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.000
So these are now
in beta and they

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:39.540
allow you to talk to instances
obviously within a VPC.

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:43.590
So GCE or GKE, things like
Cloud Memorystore that

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:46.350
are also running within a VPC.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:49.230
And then even on-prem, you
can go through compute running

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:51.970
in that VPC on to on-prem.

00:23:51.970 --> 00:23:53.610
So again, you configure
at deploy time,

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:57.880
you say gCloud functions
deploy with a VPC connector.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:03.700
And that will let you go through
that connector to that compute.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:04.210
OK.

