00:28:02.930 --> 00:28:04.235
Back to the slides, please.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:09.550
OK.

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:11.980
So there are a couple
of other issues

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:13.700
that we may face along the way.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:16.690
So we talked about this
concept of least privilege

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:19.450
within GCP of your
function only being

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:23.860
able to talk to a Cloud
SQL instance and not a GCS

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:26.030
instance, for instance.

00:28:26.030 --> 00:28:27.550
So there are a
couple of other ways

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:29.890
that data may be
exfiltrated from your app.

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:31.810
For instance, that
processContent

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:34.300
function, instead of sending--

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:37.100
making that HTTP
request to saveData.

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.600
Maybe it's going to say, oh,
send it to evil.com and steal

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:42.010
all of that data.

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.440
And that could be caused by
something like a remote code

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.420
execution, or maybe you have--

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:49.960
that the name of that URL
is in a variable and someone

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:52.590
somehow got in there
and changed it.

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:54.780
And another similar
one is, maybe

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.810
the path is the same where
you're still storing data

00:28:57.810 --> 00:29:00.870
correctly, but now someone
has dumped your environment

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:02.610
and is saving that
to the database.

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:04.620
And now your data
is stored somewhere

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:07.860
where it shouldn't and maybe you
have another thing that says--

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:09.947
you have another function
that's readContent,

00:29:09.947 --> 00:29:12.030
and now they can read the
content of your database

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:13.320
username and password.

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:15.420
There are a number of
very interesting threats

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:17.480
along that model.

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:20.438
And a similar one that
actually could cause those

00:29:20.438 --> 00:29:21.730
is things like query injection.

00:29:21.730 --> 00:29:24.930
So hopefully everyone has
heard of you know SQL injection

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:25.620
attacks.

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:28.650
Another xkcd comic about
little Bobby Tables.

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:32.550
Don't name your kid a valid
SQL statement because things

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:34.687
will not end well.

00:29:34.687 --> 00:29:37.020
I highly recommend just reading
all of xkcd, by the way.

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:39.190
It's great stuff.

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:42.390
And so maybe we have
our saveData function,

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:43.860
and it does something like this.

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:46.270
It's like, hey, I'm going
to create this query string

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:48.270
and just save my data
directly into the database

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:51.120
and pull it in
directly from the body.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:54.960
Well, that's great until you
name the user Mike DROP TABLES

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:56.430
Posts.

00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:59.070
And then we're inserting that
directly into our database.

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:02.250
Wouldn't it be great if, hey,
we could write a bunch of code

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:04.050
to manually escape that.

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:08.730
We could even do that galaxy
brain thing of a lot of SQL--

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.370
Libraries will actually
provide a really nice way

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:15.340
of doing those queries for you
that automatically escape them.

00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:17.830
But there are actually a number
of third party providers,

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:20.310
some of whom we have here
in the room today, who

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:22.590
can make this a little easier.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:26.010
So folks like Intrinsic,
Protego, and PureSec

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:28.440
offer a number of
categories of defenses

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:31.120
for your serverless
applications.

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:33.060
So some of them
occur at build time,

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:37.710
so preventing the shipping
your functions with editor role

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:41.280
because they can say, hey,
based on data we've seen,

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:45.580
you should only need
these three privileges.

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:48.240
And then they can also do things
like check all of those TVE

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:51.300
databases to make sure that your
dependencies aren't vulnerable.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:54.000
And then they also provide a
category of runtime defenses.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:56.880
So doing the kind of web
application firewall type

00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:00.030
things to prevent SQL
injection, or restricting

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.640
read from the file system
so someone can't just

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:06.132
dump your entire process or
read all of the local files,

00:31:06.132 --> 00:31:08.340
or doing things like
controlling outbound networking.

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:10.900
So don't send data to evil.com.

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:12.700
So I'll highlight
one of the tools,

00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:16.230
and then we can have
Protego come up on stage

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:18.000
and demonstrate theirs.

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.180
So one of them is called
FunctionShield, so by PureSec.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.270
And it does a couple of things.

00:31:23.270 --> 00:31:26.700
So you can just say disable
outbound networking or disable

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:30.360
read and write from
slash temp and you

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:31.480
can do a couple of things.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:34.290
You can say, hey, I want to
actively block all of these,

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.360
or you can say hey, I only
want to alert on them.

00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:38.890
So it shows up in
Stackdriver Logging,

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:41.910
it says, hey, it looks like
this request was attempted,

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:46.470
we would have failed that if you
were in strict mode, otherwise

00:31:46.470 --> 00:31:47.640
we're just letting you know.

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:50.862
So this is more about
the letting you know

00:31:50.862 --> 00:31:52.570
and then potentially
locking things down.

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.020
So for instance, I run my
functions, most of them

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:57.510
don't need to create
child processes.

00:31:57.510 --> 00:32:00.660
And so I say, yep, alert
me on outbound HTTP,

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:04.758
but disallow read and write from
temp, disallow child processes,

00:32:04.758 --> 00:32:06.300
don't allow anyone
to read the source

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:08.260
code, that kind of thing.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:10.800
And then Hillel, do
you wanna come up?

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:13.980
He can give a demo
on Protego and what

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:16.370
they provide in this space.

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:17.250
I'll hand you that.

00:32:17.250 --> 00:32:18.160
I don't know if
you need it, but--

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:19.220
HILLEL SOLOW: I need it.

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:20.670
I was sure I'd
fall in the steps.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:22.740
That was my big vision.

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:23.370
Cool.

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:24.540
Thanks, Mike.

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:27.800
So before I hit play--

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.160
I guess I'll figure that out.

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:31.410
Before I hit play, just a
quick word about what we do.

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:33.990
I think Mike set it
up in terms of what

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:36.630
we look at in terms of
security, both at build time

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:37.530
and at runtime.

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.160
For security, one of
our particular focuses

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:44.377
is on using automated tools to
understand what the code does

00:32:44.377 --> 00:32:46.710
in order to automate all the
configuration of the things

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:47.610
that Mike just talked about.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:48.990
So try to make it so that
you don't have to sit there

00:32:48.990 --> 00:32:51.630
and configure what you
think your function should

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.670
do or shouldn't do,
but rather developers

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:57.518
write code and security can be
assured that the code can do

00:32:57.518 --> 00:32:59.310
the things it can do,
and then the security

00:32:59.310 --> 00:33:01.140
policy is going to prevent
the things that it can't do.

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:02.240
That's a big focus for us.

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:06.180
Whether it's IAM roles, runtime
policy, permissions, defense,

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:09.370
et cetera, all that is automated
in a way that's driven by code.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:10.470
So let's see a quick demo.

